## Expert Coding & Workflow Standards

> **These standards are mandatory for all contributors. For details, see the Rule & Workflow References above.**

### 1. Pull Request & Code Review Checklist
- [ ] All code is type-safe (no `any` unless justified and documented)
- [ ] No lint or TypeScript errors
- [ ] All tests pass (unit, integration, E2E as relevant)
- [ ] New/changed APIs are documented in API.md
- [ ] New/changed data files are documented in data/ and this guide
- [ ] No secrets or sensitive data in code or logs
- [ ] All user-facing text is in translations/
- [ ] Accessibility (a11y) is considered for all UI changes
- [ ] Security best practices are followed (input validation, error handling, etc.)
- [ ] Documentation references in this guide are up to date
- [ ] Code is peer-reviewed and feedback addressed

_Example: Before merging a PR, copy this checklist into your PR description and verify each item._

### 2. Automated Quality Gates
- Use Husky pre-commit hooks for lint, type-check, and tests
- CI/CD (GitHub Actions) must block merges on errors

_Example: Set up Husky with `npx husky-init && npm install` and add `npm run lint && npm run type-check && npm test` to pre-commit._

### 3. Security & Privacy Best Practices
- Never log or commit secrets
- Use environment variables for sensitive config
- Sanitize all user input
- Regularly run `npm audit` and address vulnerabilities

_Example: Add `.env` to `.gitignore` and use `process.env.SECRET_KEY` in code._

### 4. Accessibility (a11y) Standards
- Use semantic HTML and ARIA attributes
- Test with screen readers and keyboard navigation
- Meet WCAG 2.1 AA standards

_Example: Add `aria-label` to buttons and test with VoiceOver._

### 5. Performance Guidelines
- Profile and optimize critical paths
- Use lazy loading/code splitting for large modules
- Monitor bundle size and performance regressions

_Example: Use React.lazy for large components and check bundle size with `npm run build`._

### 6. Testing Philosophy
- Practice TDD or at least write tests for all new features/bugfixes
- Mock external services in tests
- Maintain high test coverage, but focus on meaningful tests

_Example: Add a test in `components/__tests__/` for every new component._

### 7. Error Handling & Logging
- Use centralized error logging (e.g., Sentry)
- Never swallow errors silently
- Provide user-friendly error messages and fallback UI

_Example: Use try/catch in async functions and log errors with context._

### 8. Documentation-Driven Development
- Write or update docs before or alongside code changes
- Use diagrams (e.g., Mermaid) for complex flows
- Keep onboarding docs up to date

_Example: Add a section to API.md when adding a new endpoint._

### 9. Onboarding & Knowledge Sharing
- Encourage pair programming and code walkthroughs
- Maintain a “How to get help” section (Slack, Issues, etc.)
- Document tribal knowledge in docs/ or a dedicated FAQ

_Example: Add common troubleshooting steps to docs/README.md._

### 10. Continuous Improvement
- Regularly review and refactor code for clarity and simplicity
- Hold retrospectives to improve workflow and standards
- Encourage feedback on the developer guide itself

_Example: After each release, review this guide and update as needed._ 